      ":question/id": "cc557b9f-d58f-5e05-866d-71dbd4545f30",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+3 y}{x}\\,,\\ x\\ne 0\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{x+3 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&={x}^{2}(\\frac{x+3 y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=x(x+3 y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&={x}^{2}+3 x y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- 3 x)}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{x^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 3 x y&=x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- 3 x$ and $Q(x)=x^{2}.$",
